everyone this is kevin from the chess  website com2 they're going to be  continued our coverage of the 2014 chess  rapid world championships if you're not  too familiar with the format's kind of  covering again they play three days of  rapid chess that is 15 minutes per game  or permanent per game and you also get  10 seconds per increment for each move  that you make so you can't get more than  15 minutes in the particular game of a  very very quick chest they play five  matches a day for three straight days to  crown a champion to a lot of chests from  these players they're getting to play a  lot of different players that they  usually don't get to play and even if  you're the number one player in the  world which we're gonna be going over  one of his games right now that doesn't  necessarily mean that you are the best  rapid or blitz player and there's a lot  of players that they can lay some traps  you can fall into some very easy  mistakes that you just don't think about  in the heat of the moment so I thought  it'd be good to go over a game from  around 3:00 from Magnus cross in the top  player in the world right now just won  the world championships against Vladimir  Potkin a Russian Grandmaster very very  good chess player in his own right so  Magnus Carlsen is gonna be playing with  the white pieces and we have Vladimir  Potkin gonna be playing as the black  pieces Magnus Carlsen decides to start  the game out with pawn to e4 makes me  all warm and fuzzy when I see that very  aggressive opening line for Magnus  Carlsen I really enjoyed watching Magnus  Carlsen when he is playing very  aggressive pawn to c5 the Sicilian  defense Knight to f3 very common moves  from both side after the exchange we do  see the tamanna variation the pawn here  to e6 and then Knight here to c6 Knight  to c3 Queen c7 normal moves in the  tamanna variation pawn to a6 very  interesting I grew up studying this line  and it was always bishop here to e2  castling on the kingside Magnus Carlsen  goes for a different approach and he  actually plays pawn here to a3 somewhat  of a waiting move does stop his opponent  from playing Knight here to b4 which is  fine so continues with Knight f6 pawn  here to f4 so you can already see Magnus  Carlsen has a very different align that  he's thinking about more of an  aggressive on the Kings side maybe  castle on the Queen side he still could  cast on the King  sign have a you know ruk behind here on  f1 supporting this pawn pushing forward  but it does look like you know his  opponent is gonna be somewhat forced to  castle on the kingside he can kind of go  all-in on the kingside focus on castling  on the Queen side so this he's kind of  taking the variation that that I'm  familiar with and kind of flipped it on  his head and kind of made it more of an  aggressive line which is something you  may want to try to do especially if it's  a rapid game where your opponent may not  be super familiar with it those are the  types of small little incremental  advantages that you can gain because  Magnus Carlsen is familiar with pretty  much every opening a variation much more  so than I would imagine of Vladimir  Potkin we now see the knight exchange so  Queen here to d4 could have taken with  other pieces but this does give him an  aggressive Queen and allows him to  castle on the Queen side that much  faster Knight here to a g4 interesting  variation from here magnus carlsen plays  Queen here to b6 bishop here to d6  pushing forward with a 5  I do like pushing forward with your  central pawns so anytime you can get  your pawns ePHI VAT d5 or even pushing a  little bit further it really starts to  choke your opponent black really hasn't  made much movement with his pawns past  this 6th rank so eventually you can  start to choke his opponent and really  wear down on all the different options  this is a very good move to kind of push  the spatial advantage that Magnus  Carlsen has we do see the knight take  and then the Queen come back here  interesting to note that Magnus Carlsen  did give his opponent the double bishop  pair although it is very difficult  somewhat for for black to utilize that  he doesn't have his LightSquared bishop  even remotely into the game he does need  to get some king sized safety the bishop  is gonna come back here to e7 it was  being attacked we see the castling on  the the Queen side so again from both  sides black really wants to castle on  the King side he wants to give his life  square bishop involved into the action  because it's not doing a whole heck of a  lot he wants to bring his rook over here  to c8 semi-open file you always want  your rooks very very active the more  squares that they can move the buddy are  they are going to be from White's  perspective he knows that blacks going  to castle on the Kings side so he wants  to  just push everything in the kitchen sink  at this kingside start pushing his pawns  start bringing his rook over he doesn't  really matter start throwing his queen  over here a centralized this knight  bring it over to the King side it's not  doing too much on c3 right here so those  are that's kind of what both sides are  gonna be looking to do in this chess  game now that's always interesting when  you do chess commentary sometimes the  opponents don't do and you kind of have  to try to figure out what they're doing  or sometimes is just a huge blunder and  it's very obvious sometimes they do  exactly what you feel like they should  do sometimes that makes you feel smart  but you know it is what it is so in this  particular case we see black play pawn  here to b5 pushing forward on the Queen  side recognized the his opponents  already committed to the Queen side with  his queenside castle does also open up  the door for his bishop to come here to  b7 does need to get that involved into  the action again bishop pair doesn't do  too much if you don't have both bishops  to actually involved into the game night  here to e4 swinging it over to the King  side again on the Queen side is kind of  just sitting there is a defense mode and  Magnus Carlsen the way he's played so  far he's not looking to play defense  mode because if you're playing Vladimir  Potkin Russian Grandmaster that's pretty  good at chess but Magnus Carlsen the  number one chess player in the world he  needs to come out of this with a win  he's not playing for a draw and he's  definitely not playing for a second  place flex going to castle on the Kings  side Bishop here a to d3 bishop here to  b7 Knight here to f6 pretty interesting  doesn't really matter how black attacks  this he could attack with his bishop he  could attack with his pawn and that  would actually be okay for Magnus  Carlsen the compensation that he would  get by opening up this G file you know  in this particular game he feels like it  would be worth it  Vladimir decides to go and take with his  bishop the pawn recaptures an  interesting move in the particular game  he plays pawn to g6 and this is  interesting because this pawn on G on f6  is very very dangerous it has some  really dangerous outpost the Queen can  come up here to h6 and then a quick  checkmate here he could have easily  taken with his pawn here on f6 it's not  quite clear  how magnus carlsen could actually  continue to attack this queen here to g3  king over here to h8 he could always now  bring his rooks over here to kind of  counter-attack on the kingside no longer  is it a strong attack for whites but now  of a counter-attack for black so you  know he could have easily taken this  pawn to material and not had to worry  about these outposts but instead he  plays pawn here to g6 definitely a  questionable move in the particular game  pawn to f5 kind of breaking this up  there's no reason to have doubled pawns  go ahead and attack your opponent maybe  you can have another weakness come up on  the board the Queen coming back here to  d8 someone interesting Queen to g5 I  feel like this kind of a mistake by  Magnus Carlsen you could have easily  played rook over here to f1 this makes a  lot of sense it doesn't really matter  you know let's say we have Queen take  here on f6 and then this pawn takes here  on g6 potentially we have Queen coming  back here to g7 take and then the King  over here again this pawns being  protected by this Bishop here on d3 this  is gonna be very good for Magnus Carlsen  and you know instead he decides to play  a little bit different place Queen here  to g5 pretty aggressive although I think  he could have found something better if  he had a little bit more time rook over  here to c8 yes I do like this move  because it's a semi-open square you need  to get this rook involved but you only  want to play this if it's safe to play  and I don't think he realizes that the  number one play in the world is  completely breathing down his neck on  the King side and he really needs to  focus over there because he's in a lot  of hurt so Magnus Carlsen is just gonna  start gobbling up material pawn takes  there on g6 the pawn recaptures and then  Bishop here to g6 all of a sudden he  it's getting pretty uncomfortable for  black right now as far as what he's  gonna do  i plays Queen here to f6 I don't really  like that move I'm not really sure where  else he's supposed to go I guess he  could take with his pawn here on g6 and  then pawn too you know or the Queen  takes there on g6 it's still kind of  messy not to be confused with the  Argentinian soccer player that scored  earlier today but from here we see the  Queen take on f6 and then a bishop take  on fh7 the King come over here to h8 and  then Queen to h5 putting a lot of  pressure there is a discovered attack  once this Bishop moves we have King here  to a g7 rook up here to d7 a double  attack here attacking both the king and  the bishop right here there's not again  why it has just too many attackers in  this particular game he now plays rook  here to f7 but now Bishop coming back  here it's gonna be a mistake to go ahead  and take with his you know rook because  after you know Queen come back here to  f4 right away  forcing the King to move but then we  actually see the rook take but again  that just loses the Queen here to h7 and  then the roofs gonna fall and all of a  sudden if you kind of look at the board  White's attacking the bishop here on b7  it's attacking the rook there's no way  to kind of defend both of those on board  he can also play rook here to f1 if  blacks not careful he can't defend  everything White's already up white  material on board with two pawns he has  to pass pawns over here so in this  particular case black just decided to go  ahead and resign great game by Magnus  Carlsen and really applying a lot of  pressure to his opponent one little  small error by his opponents and all of  a sudden Magnus Carlsen went on to  dominate the game so hopefully you guys  enjoyed this always fun to watch rapid  chess because they're playing at such a  high level with not a lot of time on the  clock so hopefully you guys are enjoying  watching the games maybe even learning  something along the way  watching some of the best chess players  around the world play fast chess so  thanks for watching I'll see you guys in  the next video make sure you also check  out the chess website comm for more  coverage of both the rapid and the Bliss  championship

